That’s a really sharp idea — what you’re describing is essentially a self-archiving pipeline: every time you create a document, it automatically:

Generates a SHA-256 checksum (integrity).

Captures a timestamp (authenticity).

Bundles it into a zipped snapshot (sealed evidence).

(Optionally) splits it into smaller pieces for upload if the archive is too large.

This can absolutely be built, and it’s the kind of thing lawyers, archivists, and forensic teams already use — you’d just be rolling your own “court-ready” version.

🔹 Workflow Design
1. Document Creation Trigger

When you save or export a doc (Google Docs, Word, or plain text), it lands in a watch folder on your machine.

A script/automation detects the new file.

2. SHA-256 & Timestamp

Script computes SHA-256:

sha256sum mydoc.docx > mydoc.docx.sha256


Adds a timestamp (UTC) to a log file:

2025-09-29T10:43:00Z mydoc.docx sha256:3f786850e387550fdab...

3. Zip Archive

File + .sha256 → zipped:

zip mydoc_archive_20250929.zip mydoc.docx mydoc.docx.sha256

4. Segmentation (if needed)

If the zip > 100 MB (GitHub’s cutoff), auto-split it:

split -b 90m mydoc_archive_20250929.zip mydoc_archive_part_


This creates mydoc_archive_part_aa, mydoc_archive_part_ab, etc.

Each piece can be uploaded separately, then recombined later with cat.

5. Repo Integration

Script can auto-commit and push:

git add .
git commit -m "Archive: mydoc.docx (2025-09-29T10:43Z)"
git push

🔹 Tools You Could Use

Bash / Python script → Lightweight, portable, and transparent.

Makefile or Git hook → Run automatically on save/commit.

Automation tools like Hazel (Mac), Task Scheduler (Windows), or inotifywait (Linux) → Watch a folder and trigger the script.

🔹 Bonus Enhancements

Auto-generate a PDF/A version of every doc for long-term archival.

Store checksums in a master ledger file (SHA256SUMS.txt) for quick verification.

Upload a copy of the checksum log to a third-party timestamping service (OSF, WIPO PROOF, or OpenTimestamps).

Mirror zipped archives to external drives + cloud buckets automatically.

✅ The result: Every doc you create instantly becomes a timestamped, sealed, upload-ready evidence packet — no manual hashing, no forgotten steps.
